<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Torres Strait Islanders</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Torres Strait Islanders are the Indigenous Melanesian peoples of the islands of the Torres Strait between Cape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> in Queensland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, with cultures distinct from those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians</a>. Annexed to Queensland in the 1870s, the islands are part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. The Mabo case of 1992, brought by Meriam people, established native title in Australian law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torres-strait-islanders.jpg" alt="Torres Strait Islanders" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kelisi at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TorresStraitIslandsMap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Torres Strait Islanders are predominantly of Melanesian descent and share some genetic and cultural traits with the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>. Archaeological, linguistic and folk-history evidence suggests that the core of Island culture is Papuo-Austronesian. Before colonisation the Islanders were not a homogeneous group and did not regard themselves as a single people.</p><p>Sources agree on five geographical or cultural divisions, though they name them differently; one Queensland government publication lists the Top Western, Mid-Western, Lower Western, Central and Eastern Islanders. Oral history tells of four brothers, Malo, Sagai, Kulka and Siu, who paddled from Cape York to the central and eastern islands. Malo settled on Mer and set out rules for living that combined religion and law, which Eddie Mabo presented in the Mabo native title case in 1992.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> reached the islands in 1871, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> party led by Samuel Macfarlane and Archibald Wright Murray arrived on Erub (Darnley Island) on 1 July. The elder Dabad received the Bible Macfarlane offered, an event Islanders call the Coming of the Light and celebrate every year on 1 July. In June 1875 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a> epidemic killed about a quarter of the population, and up to 80 per cent on some islands, because Islanders had no immunity to European diseases.</p><p>Missionaries sometimes banned traditional sacred objects. In 1898 and 1899 the British anthropologist Alfred Cort Haddon collected about 2,000 objects, now the Haddon Collection at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. Interest in old ways revived from the 1960s and 1970s, helped by Margaret Lawrie's recorded stories. Until the late twentieth century the Islanders were administered through elected councils, and since 1994 the Torres Strait Regional Authority, with 20 elected representatives, has overseen the islands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Of the 133 islands, only 38 are inhabited, and the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 4,124 people living on them; many more Torres Strait Islander people live on the Australian mainland. Two Indigenous languages are spoken, Kalaw Lagaw Ya in the west and centre and Meriam Mir in the east, along with the English-based Torres Strait Creole. Islanders have long farmed as well as hunted and gathered, and dugong, turtle and fishing remain central to a seafaring tradition.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and dance carry oral history, and each island group has its own performances, with headdresses called dhari. Islanders are the only people known to make turtleshell masks. Their spiritual system includes the stories of the Tagai, and most Islanders are Christian, combining the faith with the customs known as Ailan Kastom. The five-pointed star on their flag represents the five cultural groups.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Torres Strait Islanders” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of Australia grew out of six British colonies founded between 1788, when the First Fleet established a penal settlement at Sydney Cove, and 1859, when Queensland was separated from New South Wales, on a continent inhabited by Aboriginal and <b>Torres Strait Islander</b> peoples for at least 50,000 years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Aboriginal Australians are the Indigenous peoples of the Australian mainland and many of its islands, apart from the distinct <b>Torres Strait Islanders</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The first recording was made on a wax cylinder in 1898 by Charles Samuel Myers and Alfred Cort Haddon during an expedition to record the music of the <b>Torres Strait Islanders</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Taba Naba&quot; is a traditional children's song of the Torres Strait Islands, just north of the Australian mainland, sung in Meriam Mir, a language of the <b>Torres Strait Islanders</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/taba-naba">Taba Naba</a></li></ul></section>
